WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.470
Every insertion makes the table fuller.

2
00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.090
Chains get longer.

3
00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.137
Probe sequences get longer.

4
00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.909
Sooner or later the table is

5
00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:12.500
too small for its keys.

6
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:14.120
What should it do?

7
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.500
The same thing a dynamic array does

8
00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:17.660
build a bigger one

9
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.250
and move everything over.

10
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.901
In this last video on hashing we'll

11
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.708
see what the load factor measures

12
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.164
how a table grows without

13
00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:26.725
losing a single key

14
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.076
and why a put is still constant

15
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:30.270
time on average.

16
00:00:30.850 --> 00:00:33.377
The load factor is the number of entries

17
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.940
divided by the number of slots.

18
00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.620
Ten keys in seven slots

19
00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:40.290
one point four three.

20
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.310
It is the number that controls the cost.

21
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.250
For chaining

22
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.490
it is the average length of a chain.

23
00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.070
For open addressing

24
00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:51.152
it is the fraction of slots in use

25
00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.347
and we saw that the cost of a miss

26
00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.040
explodes as it nears one.

27
00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:59.220
So every hash table has a threshold

28
00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.620
the largest load factor it allows.

29
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:04.020
When an insertion crosses it

30
00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.510
the table grows.

31
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.657
Our chained map starts with seven buckets

32
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.850
and a threshold of three quarters.

33
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.870
Insert keys one by one.

34
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.950
At the sixth key

35
00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.622
six over seven crosses three quarters

36
00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.896
and the table grows from seven

37
00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.150
slots to seventeen

38
00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.200
the next prime after twice seven.

39
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.200
At thirteen entries it grows again

40
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.360
to thirty-seven.

41
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.120
At twenty-eight, to seventy-nine.

42
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.030
At sixty, to one hundred sixty-three.

43
00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.036
Each time, the size roughly doubles

44
00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:40.550
so the load factor drops

45
00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:41.850
back to about a third

46
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.700
and the cycle starts again.

47
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.790
Growing is not just copying.

48
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:49.400
Every entry has to be hashed again.

49
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.960
Remember: the index of a key is its hash

50
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:55.540
code modulo the table size.

51
00:01:55.540 --> 00:01:58.460
Forty-four is in slot two of seven.

52
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:00.560
In seventeen slots

53
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.430
forty-four belongs in slot ten.

54
00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:04.650
If we copied the old array into

55
00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.070
the new one as it is

56
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.430
a lookup would go to slot ten, find nothing

57
00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.890
and report that forty-four is missing.

58
00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.650
The entry would be there

59
00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.540
in the wrong place, for ever.

60
00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:18.960
So a rehash visits every entry

61
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.140
computes its index in the new table

62
00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.340
and inserts it.

63
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:24.930
That is why it is called rehashing.

64
00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:28.080
A rehash touches every entry

65
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.420
so one put can cost a lot.

66
00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:33.920
Yet puts are constant time on average.

67
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.150
You have seen this argument before.

68
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.736
The table grows geometrically.

69
00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.898
Each rehash moves at most the

70
00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:42.028
entries that are present

71
00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:44.017
and the previous rehash happened when

72
00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.390
the table was less than half as big.

73
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:47.610
Add them all up

74
00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.740
fewer than two moves per entry, in total.

75
00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:52.320
We counted it.

76
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.979
A thousand puts, seven rehashes

77
00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:56.308
nine hundred eighty-four

78
00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.800
entries moved in total.

79
00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.730
Not even one move per put.

80
00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:02.529
This is the same argument as the dynamic

81
00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.350
array in the first chapter

82
00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.550
occasional expensive operations

83
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:09.120
paid for by the many cheap ones.

84
00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.350
Where should the threshold be?

85
00:03:11.350 --> 00:03:12.456
It is a trade.

86
00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:15.036
A low threshold keeps lookups fast

87
00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.870
but wastes memory on empty slots

88
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:19.660
and rehashes more often.

89
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.699
A high threshold saves memory but

90
00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.900
makes every operation slower

91
00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.063
and for open addressing it is dangerous.

92
00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:30.493
That is why chaining tables usually

93
00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:32.000
use about three quarters

94
00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:34.890
and probing tables stay below a half.

95
00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.880
Three mistakes to avoid.

96
00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.750
Copying instead of rehashing, as we saw.

97
00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.925
Growing by a constant amount

98
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:44.220
instead of a factor

99
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.240
which makes puts linear on average

100
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:48.070
as in the first chapter.

101
00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:50.669
And forgetting that tombstones count

102
00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.848
a table full of tombstones needs a

103
00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.220
rebuild even if few keys remain.

104
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.890
To sum up.

105
00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.930
The load factor is entries over slots.

106
00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:01.770
When it crosses the threshold

107
00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:05.200
build a bigger table and rehash every entry.

108
00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:07.996
Because the table grows geometrically

109
00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.447
the rebuilds cost constant

110
00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.430
time per put on average.

111
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.530
That completes maps.

112
00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:15.810
Hashing gives constant time on average

113
00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.135
but no order

114
00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:18.823
the balanced trees of the previous

115
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:20.770
chapter kept the keys sorted

116
00:04:20.770 --> 00:04:23.090
at a cost of log n.

117
00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:26.370
Next, a structure for finding the smallest

118
00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.380
the heap.

119
00:04:28.007 --> 00:04:30.487
Now rehashing in Python.

120
00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.937
At the end of every put that added a key

121
00:04:33.937 --> 00:04:35.497
we check the load factor.

122
00:04:35.557 --> 00:04:37.442
If the size is above the threshold

123
00:04:37.497 --> 00:04:38.827
times the capacity

124
00:04:38.897 --> 00:04:41.082
we rehash into the next prime

125
00:04:41.157 --> 00:04:43.347
after twice the capacity.

126
00:04:43.697 --> 00:04:46.632
A threshold of none turns resizing off.

127
00:04:46.707 --> 00:04:48.653
We used that to show the long chains

128
00:04:48.707 --> 00:04:50.217
in the earlier videos.

129
00:04:50.797 --> 00:04:52.323
The rehash first records

130
00:04:52.387 --> 00:04:53.947
what it is about to do

131
00:04:53.947 --> 00:04:55.627
how many entries there are

132
00:04:55.627 --> 00:04:57.327
what the old capacity was

133
00:04:57.327 --> 00:04:59.557
and what the new capacity is.

134
00:04:59.907 --> 00:05:01.944
Then it makes empty buckets

135
00:05:02.017 --> 00:05:04.131
and for every old entry computes

136
00:05:04.197 --> 00:05:06.897
the index again, with the new capacity

137
00:05:06.897 --> 00:05:07.757
and appends it.

138
00:05:07.757 --> 00:05:11.107
A counter keeps the number of entries moved.

139
00:05:11.687 --> 00:05:12.977
A thousand puts

140
00:05:12.977 --> 00:05:15.117
starting from seven buckets.

141
00:05:15.117 --> 00:05:18.477
The first rehash happens at six entries

142
00:05:18.477 --> 00:05:20.237
seven to seventeen.

143
00:05:20.237 --> 00:05:23.617
Then thirteen entries, to thirty-seven.

144
00:05:23.617 --> 00:05:25.607
Twenty-eight, to seventy-nine.

145
00:05:25.957 --> 00:05:27.899
Seven rehashes in all

146
00:05:27.987 --> 00:05:29.416
ending at one thousand three

147
00:05:29.467 --> 00:05:31.067
hundred sixty-one buckets.

148
00:05:31.067 --> 00:05:33.287
Nine hundred eighty-four entries moved

149
00:05:33.287 --> 00:05:35.487
which is point nine eight per put.

150
00:05:35.487 --> 00:05:38.437
The final load factor is point seven three.

151
00:05:38.787 --> 00:05:40.588
A test pins these numbers

152
00:05:40.657 --> 00:05:42.570
and also checks that the work stays below

153
00:05:42.617 --> 00:05:44.507
one and a half moves per put.

154
00:05:45.087 --> 00:05:47.357
Python's own dictionary does the same

155
00:05:47.357 --> 00:05:48.497
with its own numbers.

156
00:05:48.497 --> 00:05:50.237
Add keys one at a time

157
00:05:50.237 --> 00:05:52.557
and watch the dictionary's memory size

158
00:05:52.557 --> 00:05:56.517
it changes when the first, sixth, eleventh

159
00:05:56.517 --> 00:05:57.237
twenty-second

160
00:05:57.237 --> 00:05:59.927
forty-third and eighty-sixth key arrives.

161
00:06:00.277 --> 00:06:02.547
The table has eight slots at first

162
00:06:02.547 --> 00:06:05.487
and five of them are usable, two thirds.

163
00:06:05.487 --> 00:06:08.587
When the sixth key comes, it grows

164
00:06:08.587 --> 00:06:10.927
sixteen slots, ten usable.

165
00:06:10.927 --> 00:06:13.465
Then thirty-two, with twenty-one usable.

166
00:06:13.527 --> 00:06:15.444
Then sixty-four and one

167
00:06:15.527 --> 00:06:16.517
hundred twenty-eight.

168
00:06:16.867 --> 00:06:19.088
So CPython keeps its load

169
00:06:19.177 --> 00:06:22.558
factor below two thirds, and when it grows

170
00:06:22.637 --> 00:06:24.068
it roughly triples the number

171
00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:25.417
of used entries

172
00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:27.347
rounded up to a power of two.

173
00:06:27.697 --> 00:06:29.741
Since Python three point seven

174
00:06:29.807 --> 00:06:31.446
a dictionary also keeps its

175
00:06:31.507 --> 00:06:33.087
keys in insertion order.

176
00:06:33.087 --> 00:06:35.627
It stores the entries in a compact array

177
00:06:35.687 --> 00:06:37.322
and the sparse table only

178
00:06:37.387 --> 00:06:39.097
holds indexes into it.

179
00:06:39.677 --> 00:06:41.707
If you know the final size

180
00:06:41.707 --> 00:06:43.674
you can skip every rehash.

181
00:06:43.747 --> 00:06:45.546
Start with a table big enough for the number

182
00:06:45.587 --> 00:06:47.847
of entries divided by the threshold

183
00:06:47.847 --> 00:06:49.557
rounded up to a prime.

184
00:06:49.907 --> 00:06:52.119
For a thousand entries and a threshold

185
00:06:52.177 --> 00:06:53.655
of three quarters

186
00:06:53.737 --> 00:06:55.874
that is one thousand three hundred

187
00:06:55.937 --> 00:06:57.017
sixty-one buckets.

188
00:06:57.077 --> 00:06:58.791
The same thousand puts now

189
00:06:58.857 --> 00:07:00.957
trigger no rehash at all

190
00:07:00.957 --> 00:07:02.707
and move no entries.

191
00:07:03.287 --> 00:07:05.210
Mistakes to avoid.

192
00:07:05.317 --> 00:07:07.297
Forgetting to recompute the index

193
00:07:07.357 --> 00:07:09.447
of each entry during a rehash.

194
00:07:09.517 --> 00:07:11.460
Growing by a fixed number of slots

195
00:07:11.517 --> 00:07:13.477
instead of by a factor.

196
00:07:13.477 --> 00:07:16.177
And counting only live entries

197
00:07:16.177 --> 00:07:18.287
when tombstones fill the table.

198
00:07:18.867 --> 00:07:19.977
To recap

199
00:07:19.977 --> 00:07:22.737
check the load factor after every put

200
00:07:22.737 --> 00:07:25.508
rehash every entry into a bigger table

201
00:07:25.577 --> 00:07:27.599
and the cost per put stays

202
00:07:27.677 --> 00:07:29.517
constant on average.

203
00:07:29.517 --> 00:07:31.557
That ends the hash tables.

204
00:07:31.557 --> 00:07:33.707
Next: heaps.

